(3,'🟡','lru_ordereddict','LRU cache with collections.OrderedDict. Implement class `LRUCache(capacity)` (1 <= capacity <= 3000) with `get(key)` and `put(key, value)`, both O(1) on average. `get` returns the value, or -1 when the key is absent, and a successful `get` makes the key the most recently used. `put` on a new key inserts it as most recently used; `put` on an existing key replaces the value and also makes it most recently used; after an insert that leaves more than `capacity` keys, evict the least recently used key (a failed `get` changes nothing). `put` returns None. Use `move_to_end` and `popitem(last=False)` and say why a plain dict (insertion order only) is not enough. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). The hand-built doubly linked list version is Q{Q:lru_dll} (the linked-list idea is Part 6 Q30; sentinel pointer practice is Part 6 Q29). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.',
 [({'init': [2], 'ops': [['put', [1, 1]], ['put', [2, 2]], ['get', [1]], ['put', [3, 3]], ['get', [2]], ['put', [4, 4]], ['get', [1]], ['get', [3]], ['get', [4]]]}, [None, None, 1, None, -1, None, -1, 3, 4]), ({'init': [1], 'ops': [['put', [5, 10]], ['put', [5, 20]], ['get', [5]], ['put', [6, 1]], ['get', [5]], ['get', [6]]]}, [None, None, 20, None, -1, 1])],"Design-problem lists and guides (a LeetCode Discuss post '10 Essential Design Problems for DSA Interviews', talentd.in design-topic pages, walkccc design-problem index, a designgurus snapshot-array write-up, techinterview.org LRU-cache and rate-limiter posts, InterviewBit LRU blog; search snippets only, not opened)"),
(3,'🟡','lru_dll','LRU cache from scratch: a doubly linked list with sentinels plus a dict. Implement class `LRUCache(capacity)` (1 <= capacity <= 3000) with `get(key)`, `put(key, value)`, `order()` and `size()`; do NOT use OrderedDict, and write the node class and the two helpers `_unlink(node)` and `_push_front(node)` yourself, with a dummy head and a dummy tail so no pointer move needs an `if node is None` case. Rules: `get` returns the value or -1 and makes a found key the most recently used; `put` inserts or updates a key as most recently used and, when an insert would exceed `capacity`, first evicts the least recently used key (store the key inside the node so the evicted node can be deleted from the dict). `order()` returns the keys from most recently used to least recently used, `size()` the number of stored keys; `put` returns None. State why each of the four pointer assignments of an unlink matters, why a singly linked list cannot unlink in O(1), and say as a stated concept only (not code) what a lock around `get` and `put` would protect: both methods mutate the list, so even `get` is a write. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.',
 [({'init': [3], 'ops': [['put', [1, 'a']], ['put', [2, 'b']], ['put', [3, 'c']], ['order', []], ['get', [1]], ['order', []], ['put', [4, 'd']], ['order', []], ['get', [2]], ['size', []]]}, [None, None, None, [3, 2, 1], 'a', [1, 3, 2], None, [4, 1, 3], -1, 3]), ({'init': [1], 'ops': [['put', [1, 1]], ['put', [2, 2]], ['get', [1]], ['get', [2]], ['order', []]]}, [None, None, -1, 2, [2]])],"Design-problem lists and guides (a LeetCode Discuss post '10 Essential Design Problems for DSA Interviews', talentd.in design-topic pages, walkccc design-problem index, a designgurus snapshot-array write-up, techinterview.org LRU-cache and rate-limiter posts, InterviewBit LRU blog; search snippets only, not opened)"),
(3,'🟡','time_kv',"Time-based key-value store with bisect. Implement class `TimeMap()` with `set(key, value, timestamp)` (returns None) and `get(key, timestamp)`. `get` returns the value stored for `key` with the LARGEST stored timestamp that is <= `timestamp`, or the empty string '' when the key is unknown or every stored timestamp is greater. For one key the `set` timestamps are strictly increasing (1 <= timestamp <= 10^7), so each key keeps two parallel lists that stay sorted; `get` must use `bisect_right` (explain why bisect_left would be wrong when the timestamp equals a stored one: compare Part 5 Q11) and cost O(log m) for m values under that key, `set` O(1). Up to 2*10^5 operations. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [], 'ops': [['set', ['love', 'high', 10]], ['get', ['love', 10]], ['get', ['love', 15]], ['set', ['love', 'low', 20]], ['get', ['love', 5]], ['get', ['love', 19]], ['get', ['love', 20]], ['get', ['hate', 10]]]}, [None, 'high', 'high', None, '', 'high', 'low', ''])],"Design-problem lists and guides (a LeetCode Discuss post '10 Essential Design Problems for DSA Interviews', talentd.in design-topic pages, walkccc design-problem index, a designgurus snapshot-array write-up, techinterview.org LRU-cache and rate-limiter posts, InterviewBit LRU blog; search snippets only, not opened)"),
(3,'🟡','sliding_log',"Sliding-window-log rate limiter with an injected clock. Implement class `SlidingWindowLimiter(clock, limit, window)` with `allow(user)` returning True when the request is accepted. A request at time t is accepted when fewer than `limit` ACCEPTED requests of the same user have a timestamp in the half-open window (t - window, t], that is timestamp > t - window and timestamp <= t; so a request made exactly `window` seconds ago has already expired. Rejected requests are not recorded and do not count. Keep one deque of accepted timestamps per user and pop expired timestamps from the left; each request is O(1) amortised and memory is O(limit) per user. 1 <= limit <= 100, 1 <= window <= 3600. State what the boundary rule means with limit 2, window 10: accepted at t = 0 and t = 5, rejected at t = 9, accepted again at t = 10. Say, as a stated concept only, that several worker threads sharing the limiter would need a lock (or one limiter per thread plus a shared store) because the check and the append must happen together. The constructor's FIRST argument is `clock`, a zero-argument callable that returns the current time as an integer number of seconds; `init` holds the remaining constructor arguments, and ops are [time, method, [args]] with non-decreasing times: the checker sets the clock to `time` before each call. Read the time only through `clock()`, never from the `time` module. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a naive reference implementation on random operation sequences with an injected clock on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [2, 10], 'ops': [[0, 'allow', ['u']], [5, 'allow', ['u']], [9, 'allow', ['u']], [10, 'allow', ['u']], [10, 'allow', ['v']], [15, 'allow', ['u']], [16, 'allow', ['u']]]}, [True, True, False, True, True, True, False])],"Rate-limiter guides (techinterview.org 'Implement a Rate Limiter' covering token bucket, leaky bucket, fixed and sliding window; a Redis rate-limiter interview post on oneuptime.com; search snippets only, not opened)"),
(3,'🟡','token_bucket',"Token bucket rate limiter with an injected clock. Implement class `TokenBucket(clock, capacity, rate)`: the bucket holds at most `capacity` tokens (integer, 1 <= capacity <= 10^6), starts FULL when it is created (the clock reads 0 then) and gains `rate` tokens per elapsed second (integer, 0 <= rate <= 10^6) without ever exceeding `capacity`. `allow(cost=1)` first refills for the time since the previous call (the previous call time is updated on EVERY call, accepted or rejected), then returns True and removes `cost` tokens when at least `cost` tokens are available, otherwise returns False and removes nothing. Refill lazily inside `allow`: store `tokens` and `last`, no background timer. Because times and tokens are integers the arithmetic is exact; say why a float rate would make boundary tests fragile (use integers or Fraction). Explain how it differs from a sliding window (it allows a burst of `capacity` and then a steady `rate`). A call with cost greater than `capacity` can never succeed. The constructor's FIRST argument is `clock`, a zero-argument callable that returns the current time as an integer number of seconds; `init` holds the remaining constructor arguments, and ops are [time, method, [args]] with non-decreasing times: the checker sets the clock to `time` before each call. Read the time only through `clock()`, never from the `time` module. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a naive reference implementation on random operation sequences with an injected clock on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [3, 1], 'ops': [[0, 'allow', []], [0, 'allow', []], [0, 'allow', []], [0, 'allow', []], [1, 'allow', []], [1, 'allow', []], [10, 'allow', [3]], [10, 'allow', [1]], [11, 'allow', [2]]]}, [True, True, True, False, True, False, True, False, False])],"Rate-limiter guides (techinterview.org 'Implement a Rate Limiter' covering token bucket, leaky bucket, fixed and sliding window; a Redis rate-limiter interview post on oneuptime.com; search snippets only, not opened)"),
(3,'🟡','flatten_iter',"Flatten a nested list with an iterator. Implement class `NestedIterator(nested)` where `nested` is a list whose items are integers or (recursively) lists of the same kind; `next()` returns the next integer in left-to-right order and `hasNext()` says whether one remains. Empty lists, lists of empty lists and nesting such as [[], [[]], 3] must be skipped correctly: `hasNext()` must be right even before the first `next()`, so you must advance to the next integer eagerly (in the constructor and after every `next`), not lazily inside `next`. Use a stack of Python iterators (one per open list) instead of recursion or of flattening everything up front: memory O(depth), and a nesting depth of 5000 must not hit CPython's recursion limit of 1000 (a recursive flatten would). The checker never calls `next()` when `hasNext()` is False. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). Here `init` is a one-element list holding the nested list. BST iterator practice is Part 7 Q30. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [[[1, 1], 2, [1, 1]]], 'ops': [['hasNext', []], ['next', []], ['next', []], ['next', []], ['hasNext', []], ['next', []], ['next', []], ['hasNext', []]]}, [True, 1, 1, 2, True, 1, 1, False]), ({'init': [[[], [[]], 3, [[], []]]], 'ops': [['hasNext', []], ['next', []], ['hasNext', []]]}, [True, 3, False])],"Design-problem lists and guides (a LeetCode Discuss post '10 Essential Design Problems for DSA Interviews', talentd.in design-topic pages, walkccc design-problem index, a designgurus snapshot-array write-up, techinterview.org LRU-cache and rate-limiter posts, InterviewBit LRU blog; search snippets only, not opened)"),
(3,'🟢','hit_counter',"Hit counter for the last 5 minutes. Implement class `HitCounter(clock)` with `hit()` (records one hit at the current time, returns None) and `getHits()` (returns the number of hits recorded at times t' with now - 300 < t' <= now, so a hit exactly 300 seconds old is NOT counted). Several hits can share one timestamp. Keep a deque of [timestamp, count] pairs and a running total so that memory is bounded by the 300 distinct seconds in the window, and say how the design changes when one second can see a million hits (buckets, which this design already uses) and when you need the last N minutes for large N (a circular array of 300 buckets keyed by second modulo 300, with the stale-bucket check). The constructor's FIRST argument is `clock`, a zero-argument callable that returns the current time as an integer number of seconds; `init` holds the remaining constructor arguments, and ops are [time, method, [args]] with non-decreasing times: the checker sets the clock to `time` before each call. Read the time only through `clock()`, never from the `time` module. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a naive reference implementation on random operation sequences with an injected clock on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [], 'ops': [[1, 'hit', []], [2, 'hit', []], [3, 'hit', []], [4, 'getHits', []], [300, 'getHits', []], [301, 'getHits', []], [301, 'hit', []], [302, 'getHits', []], [310, 'getHits', []]]}, [None, None, None, 3, 3, 2, None, 2, 1])],"Design-problem lists and guides (a LeetCode Discuss post '10 Essential Design Problems for DSA Interviews', talentd.in design-topic pages, walkccc design-problem index, a designgurus snapshot-array write-up, techinterview.org LRU-cache and rate-limiter posts, InterviewBit LRU blog; search snippets only, not opened)"),
(2,'🟢','fixed_window',"Fixed-window counter rate limiter and its boundary burst. Implement class `FixedWindowLimiter(clock, limit, window)` with `allow(user)`: time is cut into windows [k*window, (k+1)*window) for k = t // window; a request is accepted when fewer than `limit` requests were ACCEPTED for that user in the current window (the count resets when the window index changes); rejected requests do not count. O(1) time and memory per user. Show the weakness with limit 2, window 10: accepted at t = 8, 9, 10 and 11 (four requests inside 4 seconds, double the intended rate), which a sliding window would reject; compare with the sliding window log of Q{Q:sliding_log} and the token bucket of Q{Q:token_bucket}. Say as a stated concept that a distributed version uses an atomic increment with an expiry in a shared store. The constructor's FIRST argument is `clock`, a zero-argument callable that returns the current time as an integer number of seconds; `init` holds the remaining constructor arguments, and ops are [time, method, [args]] with non-decreasing times: the checker sets the clock to `time` before each call. Read the time only through `clock()`, never from the `time` module. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a naive reference implementation on random operation sequences with an injected clock on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [2, 10], 'ops': [[8, 'allow', ['u']], [9, 'allow', ['u']], [9, 'allow', ['u']], [10, 'allow', ['u']], [11, 'allow', ['u']], [11, 'allow', ['u']], [20, 'allow', ['u']], [20, 'allow', ['v']]]}, [True, True, False, True, True, False, True, True])],"Rate-limiter guides (techinterview.org 'Implement a Rate Limiter' covering token bucket, leaky bucket, fixed and sliding window; a Redis rate-limiter interview post on oneuptime.com; search snippets only, not opened)"),
(2,'🔴','lfu','LFU cache with O(1) operations. Implement class `LFUCache(capacity)` (0 <= capacity <= 10^4) with `get(key)` and `put(key, value)`, both O(1) on average. `get` returns the value or -1. Every successful `get` and every `put` (new or existing key) counts as one use of that key; a new key starts with frequency 1. When a NEW key arrives while the cache holds `capacity` keys, first evict the key with the smallest use count and, among keys with equal counts, the least recently used one (recency counts both gets and puts); then insert. `put` on an existing key replaces the value, adds one use and refreshes its recency. With capacity 0 every `put` does nothing and every `get` returns -1. `put` returns None. Use a dict key -> (value, freq), a dict freq -> OrderedDict of keys, and a `min_freq` pointer; explain why `min_freq` can be updated in O(1) (it becomes 1 on an insert and moves from f to f + 1 only when bucket f empties). State why a heap of (freq, time) would be O(log n). The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.',
 [({'init': [2], 'ops': [['put', [1, 1]], ['put', [2, 2]], ['get', [1]], ['put', [3, 3]], ['get', [2]], ['get', [3]], ['put', [4, 4]], ['get', [1]], ['get', [3]], ['get', [4]]]}, [None, None, 1, None, -1, 3, None, -1, 3, 4]), ({'init': [0], 'ops': [['put', [0, 0]], ['get', [0]]]}, [None, -1])],"Design-problem lists and guides (a LeetCode Discuss post '10 Essential Design Problems for DSA Interviews', talentd.in design-topic pages, walkccc design-problem index, a designgurus snapshot-array write-up, techinterview.org LRU-cache and rate-limiter posts, InterviewBit LRU blog; search snippets only, not opened)"),
(2,'🟢','peeking','Peeking iterator. Implement class `PeekingIterator(it)` where `it` is a Python iterator (the checker passes `iter(list)`): you may only call `next(it)`, never index or copy it, and `list(it)` is not allowed. Methods: `peek()` returns the next element WITHOUT consuming it (calling it twice returns the same value), `next()` returns and consumes it, `hasNext()` says whether an element remains. Keep one buffered element and refill it after each `next`. The checker never calls `peek` or `next` when `hasNext()` is False. Say why the buffer must be filled eagerly (so `hasNext` is a plain flag) and the cost of one extra element of memory. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). Here `init` is a one-element list holding the list that the checker wraps with `iter()`. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.',
 [({'init': [[1, 2, 3]], 'ops': [['next', []], ['peek', []], ['next', []], ['next', []], ['hasNext', []]]}, [1, 2, 2, 3, False]), ({'init': [[7]], 'ops': [['peek', []], ['peek', []], ['hasNext', []], ['next', []], ['hasNext', []]]}, [7, 7, True, 7, False])],"Design-problem lists and guides (a LeetCode Discuss post '10 Essential Design Problems for DSA Interviews', talentd.in design-topic pages, walkccc design-problem index, a designgurus snapshot-array write-up, techinterview.org LRU-cache and rate-limiter posts, InterviewBit LRU blog; search snippets only, not opened)"),
(2,'🟢','logger',"Logger rate limiter for repeated messages. Implement class `Logger(clock)` with `shouldPrint(message)`: return True (and remember the time) when `message` was never printed before or was last printed at time p with now - p >= 10 (so exactly 10 seconds later it is allowed again); otherwise return False WITHOUT updating the remembered time. A message that was refused keeps its old print time, so a steady stream of the same message every 3 seconds prints at t = 0, 12 (the first arrival at or after 10), 24 and so on. One dict message -> last printed time gives O(1) per call; say, as a stated concept, how to bound memory for an unbounded stream of distinct messages (periodically drop entries older than 10 seconds, or keep a deque of (time, message) in arrival order). Compare the boundary with the hit counter of Q{Q:hit_counter}. The constructor's FIRST argument is `clock`, a zero-argument callable that returns the current time as an integer number of seconds; `init` holds the remaining constructor arguments, and ops are [time, method, [args]] with non-decreasing times: the checker sets the clock to `time` before each call. Read the time only through `clock()`, never from the `time` module. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a naive reference implementation on random operation sequences with an injected clock on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [], 'ops': [[1, 'shouldPrint', ['foo']], [2, 'shouldPrint', ['bar']], [3, 'shouldPrint', ['foo']], [8, 'shouldPrint', ['bar']], [10, 'shouldPrint', ['foo']], [11, 'shouldPrint', ['foo']], [12, 'shouldPrint', ['bar']], [21, 'shouldPrint', ['foo']]]}, [True, True, False, False, False, True, True, True])],"Design-problem lists and guides (a LeetCode Discuss post '10 Essential Design Problems for DSA Interviews', talentd.in design-topic pages, walkccc design-problem index, a designgurus snapshot-array write-up, techinterview.org LRU-cache and rate-limiter posts, InterviewBit LRU blog; search snippets only, not opened)"),
(2,'🟡','snapshot','Snapshot array. Implement class `SnapshotArray(length)` (1 <= length <= 5*10^4): every index starts at 0. `set(index, val)` changes the current array (returns None), `snap()` freezes the current contents and returns the snapshot id, with ids 0, 1, 2, ... in order, and `get(index, snap_id)` returns the value index had when snapshot `snap_id` was taken (snap_id is always an id already returned). Do not copy the array on `snap`: for each index keep a list of (snap_id, value) pairs recorded only when it is set, overwrite the last pair when the same index is set twice before a snapshot, and answer `get` with `bisect_right` on the snap ids (the answer is the last pair with id <= snap_id; the initial pair (0, 0) guarantees one exists). `snap` is O(1), `set` O(1), `get` O(log k). Up to 5*10^4 operations. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.',
 [({'init': [3], 'ops': [['set', [0, 5]], ['snap', []], ['set', [0, 6]], ['get', [0, 0]], ['set', [1, 9]], ['set', [1, 8]], ['snap', []], ['get', [1, 0]], ['get', [1, 1]], ['get', [0, 1]], ['get', [2, 1]]]}, [None, 0, None, 5, None, None, 1, 0, 8, 6, 0])],"Design-problem lists and guides (a LeetCode Discuss post '10 Essential Design Problems for DSA Interviews', talentd.in design-topic pages, walkccc design-problem index, a designgurus snapshot-array write-up, techinterview.org LRU-cache and rate-limiter posts, InterviewBit LRU blog; search snippets only, not opened)"),
(2,'🟡','twitter',"News feed with heaps (design Twitter). Implement class `Twitter(feed_size)` with `postTweet(user, tweet_id)`, `follow(a, b)`, `unfollow(a, b)` and `getNewsFeed(user)`. A user always sees their own tweets. `getNewsFeed` returns the ids of the at most `feed_size` most recent tweets posted by the user or anyone the user follows, newest first, where recency is the global order of the `postTweet` calls (tweet ids are arbitrary labels, not an ordering). `follow(a, b)` with a == b and `unfollow` of someone not followed do nothing; following twice is the same as once; `unfollow` stops that person's tweets (past and future) from appearing. The mutators return None. Keep each user's tweets as a list in posting order and merge the newest-first lists of the user and followees with a heap (`heapq.merge` or a heap of (negative post number, user, index)); a feed costs O(f + k log f) for f followees and k = feed_size, not a sort of all tweets. 1 <= feed_size <= 20 and up to 3*10^4 operations. Say as a stated concept how a real service would pre-compute feeds (fan-out on write versus on read). The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [3], 'ops': [['postTweet', [1, 5]], ['getNewsFeed', [1]], ['follow', [1, 2]], ['postTweet', [2, 6]], ['getNewsFeed', [1]], ['postTweet', [2, 7]], ['postTweet', [1, 8]], ['getNewsFeed', [1]], ['unfollow', [1, 2]], ['getNewsFeed', [1]]]}, [None, [5], None, None, [6, 5], None, None, [8, 7, 6], None, [8, 5]])],"Design-problem lists and guides (a LeetCode Discuss post '10 Essential Design Problems for DSA Interviews', talentd.in design-topic pages, walkccc design-problem index, a designgurus snapshot-array write-up, techinterview.org LRU-cache and rate-limiter posts, InterviewBit LRU blog; search snippets only, not opened)"),
(2,'🟡','scc_kosaraju',"Strongly connected components with Kosaraju. `n` nodes 0..n-1 (1 <= n <= 10^5) and `edges`, a list of directed [u, v] pairs (0 <= len <= 2*10^5; repeats and self loops allowed). Return the list of strongly connected components (maximal sets of nodes that can all reach each other), each component sorted ascending and the list of components sorted by smallest member. Run an iterative DFS to get the finish order, then an iterative DFS on the reversed graph in decreasing finish order; recursion is NOT allowed because a path graph of 10^5 nodes would exceed CPython's default recursion limit of 1000 (see Part 9 Q6 for the explicit-stack DFS). Say why the first pass order works (the component finishing last in the first pass is a source of the condensation DAG), and what the condensation is. The Tarjan one-pass version is Q{Q:scc_tarjan}. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a closure-based oracle (Floyd-Warshall reachability: u and v are together exactly when each reaches the other) on small inputs.",
 [({'n': 5, 'edges': [[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 0], [1, 3], [3, 4]]}, [[0, 1, 2], [3], [4]]), ({'n': 3, 'edges': [[0, 0]]}, [[0], [1], [2]]), ({'n': 4, 'edges': [[0, 1], [1, 0], [2, 3], [3, 2], [1, 2]]}, [[0, 1], [2, 3]])],'Advanced-graph interview guides (techinterview.org DFS advanced patterns and advanced graph algorithms posts, algocademy and Naukri Code360 Tarjan pages; search snippets only, not opened)'),
(2,'🔴','bridges',"Bridges in an undirected graph (critical connections). `n` nodes 0..n-1 (1 <= n <= 10^5) and `edges`, a list of undirected [u, v] pairs (0 <= len <= 2*10^5; the graph may be disconnected, may contain parallel edges and self loops). A bridge is an edge whose removal increases the number of connected components. Return the bridges as [min(u, v), max(u, v)] pairs sorted ascending, each bridge once. Use the DFS discovery time `disc` and `low` (the smallest discovery time reachable from the subtree using at most one back edge): the tree edge (p, u) is a bridge exactly when low[u] > disc[p]. Skip the PARENT EDGE BY EDGE INDEX, not by parent node, so that two parallel edges between p and u are correctly not bridges. Write it iteratively (an explicit stack of (node, parent edge, next neighbour index)): a path of 10^5 nodes exceeds CPython's default recursion limit of 1000. The articulation-point variant is Q{Q:articulation}. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against an oracle that removes each edge in turn and recounts connected components on small inputs.",
 [({'n': 5, 'edges': [[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 0], [1, 3], [3, 4]]}, [[1, 3], [3, 4]]), ({'n': 2, 'edges': [[0, 1], [0, 1]]}, []), ({'n': 6, 'edges': [[0, 1], [2, 3], [3, 4], [4, 2], [5, 5]]}, [[0, 1]])],'Advanced-graph interview guides (techinterview.org DFS advanced patterns and advanced graph algorithms posts, algocademy and Naukri Code360 Tarjan pages; search snippets only, not opened)'),
(2,'🔴','itinerary',"Reconstruct the itinerary with Hierholzer's algorithm. `tickets` is a list of [from, to] airport codes (strings of 3 uppercase letters, 1 <= len <= 300; the same ticket can appear several times). The trip starts at 'JFK' and must use every ticket exactly once; the input is guaranteed to allow at least one such trip. Return the itinerary as the list of airports (length len(tickets) + 1) that is smallest in lexicographic order of the whole list (compare airports as strings, position by position) among all valid trips. Build adjacency lists sorted in DECREASING order so `pop()` takes the smallest destination, run an iterative DFS that appends a node to the answer only when it has no unused ticket left, and reverse at the end; explain why greedy 'always take the smallest' without this post-order trick fails (it can strand tickets in a dead end). Existence tests for Eulerian trails are Part 9 Q36 and are not repeated. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a backtracking oracle that tries the destinations in sorted order and keeps the first complete trip on small inputs.",
 [({'tickets': [['JFK', 'KUL'], ['JFK', 'NRT'], ['NRT', 'JFK']]}, ['JFK', 'NRT', 'JFK', 'KUL']), ({'tickets': [['JFK', 'SFO'], ['JFK', 'ATL'], ['SFO', 'ATL'], ['ATL', 'JFK'], ['ATL', 'SFO']]}, ['JFK', 'ATL', 'JFK', 'SFO', 'ATL', 'SFO'])],'Advanced-graph interview guides (techinterview.org DFS advanced patterns and advanced graph algorithms posts, algocademy and Naukri Code360 Tarjan pages; search snippets only, not opened)'),
(2,'🟡','topo_lex',"Lexicographically smallest topological order. `n` nodes 0..n-1 (1 <= n <= 10^5) and `edges` a list of [a, b] meaning a must come before b (0 <= len <= 2*10^5; repeats allowed). Return the topological order that is smallest when compared as a list (the first node as small as possible, then the second, and so on), or [] when a cycle makes any order impossible. Kahn's algorithm with a min-heap of the nodes whose in-degree is 0 (a plain FIFO queue gives a valid order but not the smallest); O((n + m) log n). Explain why taking the smallest available node at each step is optimal here (the choice set is exactly the available nodes) and why it is NOT the right rule for 'put label 0 as early as possible, then label 1, and so on' (for that goal reverse the graph, take the LARGEST available node each time, and reverse the result). Part 9 Q9 gives any valid order and Part 9 Q33 builds the order from word constraints; this question fixes the tie rule. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against an oracle that tries all permutations in lexicographic order for n <= 6 and keeps the first valid one on small inputs.",
 [({'n': 4, 'edges': [[3, 0], [3, 1], [1, 2]]}, [3, 0, 1, 2]), ({'n': 3, 'edges': [[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 0]]}, []), ({'n': 3, 'edges': []}, [0, 1, 2])],'Advanced-graph interview guides (techinterview.org DFS advanced patterns and advanced graph algorithms posts, algocademy and Naukri Code360 Tarjan pages; search snippets only, not opened)'),
(2,'🟡','sp_coupon',"Cheapest path with one half-price edge. `n` nodes 0..n-1 (2 <= n <= 10^4) and `edges`, a list of directed [u, v, w] with integer cost 1 <= w <= 10^6 (0 <= len <= 5*10^4). Travel from node 0 to node n-1. You hold ONE coupon that you may use on at most one edge of your path, making that edge cost w // 2 (integer floor division). Return the minimum total cost, or -1 when n-1 is unreachable. Run Dijkstra on the layered state (node, coupon_used in {0, 1}) with a heap and lazy stale-entry skipping: the answer is min(dist[n-1][0], dist[n-1][1]). Say why a plain Dijkstra on the original graph cannot answer it, why positive weights keep the best walk simple, and why the same state-expansion idea handles 'at most k coupons' with k + 1 layers. The 'at most k stops' flight question is Part 9 Q34 and is not repeated. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a brute-force oracle that enumerates every simple path and subtracts the best single saving w - w // 2 on small inputs.",
 [({'n': 3, 'edges': [[0, 1, 10], [1, 2, 10], [0, 2, 25]]}, 12), ({'n': 3, 'edges': [[0, 1, 4]]}, -1), ({'n': 4, 'edges': [[0, 1, 1], [1, 3, 1], [0, 2, 3], [2, 3, 3]]}, 1)],'Advanced-graph interview guides (techinterview.org DFS advanced patterns and advanced graph algorithms posts, algocademy and Naukri Code360 Tarjan pages; search snippets only, not opened)'),
(2,'🟡','segtree_min','Segment tree for range minimum with point updates. Implement class `MinSegTree(nums)` (1 <= len <= 10^5) with `update(i, v)` (set nums[i] = v, returns None) and `query(l, r)` (the minimum of nums[l..r], both ends included, 0 <= l <= r < len). Build a bottom-up array tree of size 2n: leaves at positions n..2n-1, node i holds min(tree[2i], tree[2i+1]); `update` walks up from the leaf, `query` shrinks the half-open range [l + n, r + n + 1) and takes a node whenever l is odd or r is odd. Both operations are O(log n), the build O(n) and the memory 2n. Explain why this bottom-up form works for any n (not only powers of two) for a commutative, associative operation such as min, sum or gcd, and when you need the recursive top-down form instead (lazy propagation: Q{Q:lazy_range}, or a non-commutative merge). A plain Fenwick tree cannot answer min when values can be set to larger numbers (a prefix minimum cannot be undone, so there is no delta to add: explain in one line). The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.',
 [({'init': [[5, 3, 8, 1, 9, 2]], 'ops': [['query', [0, 5]], ['query', [2, 4]], ['update', [3, 10]], ['query', [0, 5]], ['query', [2, 4]], ['update', [0, 0]], ['query', [0, 1]]]}, [1, 1, None, 2, 8, None, 0]), ({'init': [[4]], 'ops': [['query', [0, 0]], ['update', [0, -2]], ['query', [0, 0]]]}, [4, None, -2])],'Segment-tree and Fenwick-tree interview-pattern guides (several techinterview.org posts, a mintlify Fenwick snippet page, cp-algorithms sparse-table page, designgurus range-minimum-query lesson; search snippets only, not opened)'),
(2,'🟡','sparse_table','Sparse table for static range minimum queries. `nums` has 1 <= len <= 10^5 integers (static, never updated) and `queries` is a list of up to 10^5 pairs [l, r] with 0 <= l <= r < len(nums). Return `[answers, entries]`: `answers` holds, for each query, `[minimum, index of its LEFTMOST occurrence inside l..r]`, and `entries` is the total number of cells stored in your table (level j stores one cell for every start i with i + 2^j <= n, for every level with 2^j <= n). Build `table[j][i]` = the best (value, index) pair for the block [i, i + 2^j) from two blocks of level j - 1 (compare (value, index) tuples so the leftmost index wins ties), and answer a query with k = floor(log2(r - l + 1)) from `table[k][l]` and `table[k][r - 2^k + 1]`: the two blocks overlap, which is harmless because min is idempotent (a sum would double count). Build O(n log n), query O(1); compare with the segment tree of Q{Q:segtree_min} (O(log n) query but supports updates) and say why Part 2 Q45 can also use a deque. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against slicing with min() and list.index on each query on small inputs.',
 [({'nums': [5, 2, 4, 2, 7], 'queries': [[0, 4], [2, 4], [0, 0]]}, [[[2, 1], [2, 3], [5, 0]], 11]), ({'nums': [3], 'queries': [[0, 0]]}, [[[3, 0]], 1])],'Segment-tree and Fenwick-tree interview-pattern guides (several techinterview.org posts, a mintlify Fenwick snippet page, cp-algorithms sparse-table page, designgurus range-minimum-query lesson; search snippets only, not opened)'),
(2,'🟢','coord_compress',"Coordinate compression (dense ranks). `values` is a list of 0 <= len <= 10^5 integers with |value| <= 10^18 (duplicates and negatives allowed). Return `[ranks, distinct]`: `ranks[i]` is the 1-based DENSE rank of values[i] (equal values share one rank, the smallest value gets 1, and ranks have no gaps) and `distinct` the number of distinct values. Sort the distinct values once and look each element up (a dict from value to rank, or `bisect_left` on the sorted unique list); O(n log n). Explain why Fenwick trees and counting arrays need ranks (they index by position, and 10^18 cannot index an array), why ranks must start at 1 for a Fenwick tree (index 0 is unused), and the difference from the 'rank transform' with ties that skips numbers (competition ranking). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against an O(n^2) oracle that counts, for each value, the distinct smaller values on small inputs.",
 [({'values': [40, -5, 40, 7]}, [[3, 1, 3, 2], 3]), ({'values': []}, [[], 0]), ({'values': [1000000000000000000, -1000000000000000000, 0]}, [[3, 1, 2], 3])],'Segment-tree and Fenwick-tree interview-pattern guides (several techinterview.org posts, a mintlify Fenwick snippet page, cp-algorithms sparse-table page, designgurus range-minimum-query lesson; search snippets only, not opened)'),
(2,'🟡','inversions_bit',"Count inversions with a Fenwick tree (BIT). `nums` has 0 <= len <= 10^5 integers (duplicates allowed, |value| <= 10^9). An inversion is a pair i < j with nums[i] > nums[j] (STRICTLY greater, equal values are not inversions). Return `[total, before_greater]` where `before_greater[j]` is the number of earlier elements strictly greater than nums[j] and `total` is their sum. Compress the values to ranks 1..m (Q{Q:coord_compress}), scan left to right, and for each element add `j - prefix_count(rank[j])` before inserting its rank: `prefix_count` is a BIT prefix sum, update and query both use the loops `i += i & -i` and `i -= i & -i`. O(n log n) time, O(n) space. Compare with the merge-sort counter of Part 13 Q17 (same complexity, no extra per-element information) and say what changes for 'at most' instead of 'strictly'. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against an O(n^2) double loop on small inputs.",
 [({'nums': [3, 1, 2, 3, 1]}, [5, [0, 1, 1, 0, 3]]), ({'nums': []}, [0, []]), ({'nums': [2, 2, 2]}, [0, [0, 0, 0]])],'Segment-tree and Fenwick-tree interview-pattern guides (several techinterview.org posts, a mintlify Fenwick snippet page, cp-algorithms sparse-table page, designgurus range-minimum-query lesson; search snippets only, not opened)'),
(1,'🔴','all_o1',"All-O(1) data structure. Implement class `AllOne()` with `inc(key)` (add one to the key's count, creating it at 1), `dec(key)` (subtract one; when the count reaches 0 the key disappears; `dec` of an unknown key does nothing), `getMaxKey()` and `getMinKey()`, all O(1). Both getters return '' when the structure is empty. Tie rule (the checker needs one answer): among keys with the maximum (minimum) count return the key that has held its CURRENT count the longest, that is whose count changed to this value earliest; a key that changes count joins the END of the new count's bucket. Use a doubly linked list of buckets ordered by count (dummy head and tail), each bucket holding an OrderedDict of its keys, plus a dict key -> bucket; moving a key to the neighbouring count is O(1) because the target bucket is adjacent, and an empty bucket is unlinked. `inc` and `dec` return None. Explain why a dict plus a heap would cost O(log n). The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a naive reference that stores counts and a last-change stamp and scans all keys on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [], 'ops': [['inc', ['a']], ['inc', ['b']], ['inc', ['b']], ['getMaxKey', []], ['getMinKey', []], ['inc', ['a']], ['inc', ['c']], ['getMaxKey', []], ['getMinKey', []], ['dec', ['b']], ['dec', ['b']], ['getMaxKey', []], ['dec', ['z']], ['getMinKey', []]]}, [None, None, None, 'b', 'a', None, None, 'b', 'c', None, None, 'a', None, 'c'])],"Design-problem lists and guides (a LeetCode Discuss post '10 Essential Design Problems for DSA Interviews', talentd.in design-topic pages, walkccc design-problem index, a designgurus snapshot-array write-up, techinterview.org LRU-cache and rate-limiter posts, InterviewBit LRU blog; search snippets only, not opened)"),
(1,'🟡','zigzag','Zigzag iterator over k lists. Implement class `ZigzagIterator(lists)` where `lists` is a list of 0 <= k <= 100 lists of integers (some may be empty, total size <= 10^4) with `next()` and `hasNext()`: `next()` returns the elements round-robin, one from each non-exhausted list in list order, then the second element of each, and so on; a list that runs out is simply skipped in later rounds, so [[1, 2, 3], [4], [5, 6]] gives 1, 4, 5, 2, 6, 3. Keep a deque of (list, next index) pairs for the non-empty lists: `next` pops the front, reads its element and re-appends the pair when more elements remain, O(1) per call and O(k) memory (two lists only is the classic case; the deque handles any k). The checker never calls `next()` when `hasNext()` is False. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). Here `init` is a one-element list holding `lists`. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.',
 [({'init': [[[1, 2, 3], [4], [5, 6]]], 'ops': [['next', []], ['next', []], ['next', []], ['next', []], ['hasNext', []], ['next', []], ['next', []], ['hasNext', []]]}, [1, 4, 5, 2, True, 6, 3, False]), ({'init': [[[], []]], 'ops': [['hasNext', []]]}, [False])],"Design-problem lists and guides (a LeetCode Discuss post '10 Essential Design Problems for DSA Interviews', talentd.in design-topic pages, walkccc design-problem index, a designgurus snapshot-array write-up, techinterview.org LRU-cache and rate-limiter posts, InterviewBit LRU blog; search snippets only, not opened)"),
(1,'🟡','parallel_time',"Parallel courses with durations: the minimum time to finish everything. Courses are labelled 1..n (1 <= n <= 5*10^4); `relations` is a list of [prev, next] meaning prev must be fully finished before next can start (0 <= len <= 10^5); `time[i - 1]` (1 <= time <= 10^4) is how many months course i takes; any number of courses may run at the same time. Return `[total, finish]` where `finish[i - 1]` is the earliest month at which course i can be finished (start at the latest finish among its prerequisites, or month 0) and `total` is the maximum finish; if a cycle makes some course impossible return `[-1, []]`. Use Kahn's algorithm and propagate `start[next] = max(start[next], finish[prev])`; the answer is the longest weighted path in the DAG. Part 9 Q24 is the unit-time version (minimum semesters); this one weights the nodes. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a memoised recursive oracle on small DAGs with its own cycle detection on small inputs.",
 [({'n': 3, 'relations': [[1, 3], [2, 3]], 'time': [3, 2, 5]}, [8, [3, 2, 8]]), ({'n': 2, 'relations': [[1, 2], [2, 1]], 'time': [1, 1]}, [-1, []]), ({'n': 4, 'relations': [], 'time': [4, 9, 1, 2]}, [9, [4, 9, 1, 2]])],'Advanced-graph interview guides (techinterview.org DFS advanced patterns and advanced graph algorithms posts, algocademy and Naukri Code360 Tarjan pages; search snippets only, not opened)'),
(1,'🔴','scc_tarjan',"Tarjan's strongly connected components, iteratively, with a fixed numbering. `n` nodes 0..n-1 (1 <= n <= 10^5), `edges` directed [u, v] (0 <= len <= 2*10^5; repeats and self loops allowed). Run Tarjan's one-pass algorithm: start a DFS from node 0, 1, 2, ... in that order (skipping visited nodes) and follow each node's out-edges in the ORDER THEY APPEAR in `edges`; keep `idx` (discovery number), `low`, an `on_stack` flag and the node stack; when low[u] == idx[u] pop the stack down to u and give those nodes the next component number 0, 1, 2, ... (so components are numbered in the order they are completed). Return `[comp, count, cross]`: `comp[v]` is the component number of node v, `count` the number of components, and `cross` the number of DISTINCT pairs (comp[u], comp[v]) over edges with different components (the edge count of the condensation). Explain why this numbering is a reverse topological order of the condensation (every edge u -> v has comp[u] >= comp[v]), why only nodes still ON the stack may lower `low[u]` (a node already assigned to a finished component cannot be in u's component), and why the code must be iterative (a path graph of 10^5 nodes would exceed CPython's default recursion limit of 1000). Compare with Kosaraju (Q{Q:scc_kosaraju}): one DFS instead of two plus a reversed graph. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a recursive Tarjan oracle with identical visiting order on small graphs (plus the property comp[u] >= comp[v] on every edge) on small inputs.",
 [({'n': 5, 'edges': [[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 0], [1, 3], [3, 4]]}, [[2, 2, 2, 1, 0], 3, 2]), ({'n': 3, 'edges': [[0, 1], [1, 0], [0, 2], [0, 2]]}, [[1, 1, 0], 2, 1])],'Advanced-graph interview guides (techinterview.org DFS advanced patterns and advanced graph algorithms posts, algocademy and Naukri Code360 Tarjan pages; search snippets only, not opened)'),
(1,'🔴','articulation',"Articulation points (cut vertices). `n` nodes 0..n-1 (1 <= n <= 10^5) and `edges` undirected [u, v] pairs (0 <= len <= 2*10^5; the graph may be disconnected; parallel edges and self loops may occur). A node is an articulation point when deleting it (and its edges) increases the number of connected components among the remaining nodes beyond what its own removal explains (an isolated node, or a node alone in its component, is NOT an articulation point). Return the articulation points sorted ascending. Use the discovery times and `low` values of Q{Q:bridges} with the two rules: a DFS ROOT is an articulation point when it has at least two DFS-tree children; a non-root p is one when some child u has low[u] >= disc[p] (note >=, where a bridge needs >). Write it iteratively because a path of 10^5 nodes exceeds CPython's default recursion limit of 1000. Say why >= is right for vertices and > for edges (a back edge that lands on p itself still leaves p as the cut vertex but saves the edge). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against an oracle that deletes each node in turn and recounts connected components on small inputs.",
 [({'n': 5, 'edges': [[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 0], [1, 3], [3, 4]]}, [1, 3]), ({'n': 3, 'edges': [[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 0]]}, []), ({'n': 4, 'edges': [[0, 1], [0, 2], [0, 3]]}, [0])],'Advanced-graph interview guides (techinterview.org DFS advanced patterns and advanced graph algorithms posts, algocademy and Naukri Code360 Tarjan pages; search snippets only, not opened)'),
(1,'🔴','bipartite_matching',"Maximum bipartite matching with augmenting paths (Kuhn's algorithm). `L` left nodes 0..L-1 and `R` right nodes 0..R-1 (1 <= L, R <= 300) with `edges`, a list of [left, right] pairs (0 <= len <= 3000, repeats allowed). A matching pairs each node with at most one partner along an edge. Return `[size, saturated]`: the maximum number of matched pairs and whether it equals L (every left node matched). For each left node run a DFS that tries each neighbour v not yet seen in this attempt: if v is free or its current partner can be re-matched elsewhere (recursive call), take v. Time O(L * E); the recursion depth is at most L <= 300, far below CPython's default recursion limit of 1000, so recursion is fine (a version for much larger L would need an explicit stack or Hopcroft-Karp). Explain why an augmenting path is the only way a matching can grow (Berge's theorem, stated, not proved) and the link to the earlier basic colouring check Part 9 Q21 (a graph is bipartite exactly when it has no odd cycle) and to 'assign jobs to workers' questions. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against an exhaustive oracle that tries every assignment of right nodes with a memoised DP over bitmasks (L, R <= 6) on small inputs.",
 [({'L': 3, 'R': 3, 'edges': [[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0], [2, 0]]}, [2, False]), ({'L': 2, 'R': 2, 'edges': [[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0]]}, [2, True])],'Advanced-graph interview guides (techinterview.org DFS advanced patterns and advanced graph algorithms posts, algocademy and Naukri Code360 Tarjan pages; search snippets only, not opened)'),
(1,'🔴','lazy_range',"Segment tree with lazy propagation: range add and range sum. Implement class `RangeAddSum(nums)` (1 <= len <= 10^5) with `add(l, r, v)` (add v, |v| <= 10^9, to every element of nums[l..r], both ends included; returns None) and `sum(l, r)` (the sum of nums[l..r], both ends included). Use a top-down tree where node o covers [lo, hi] and stores `sm[o]` (the true sum of its range) and `lz[o]` (an addition promised to both children but not yet pushed down): when a query or update only partly overlaps a node, `push` the pending value down first (children's sums grow by value * length and their lazy tags by value), recurse, then recompute `sm[o]` from the children. Both operations O(log n), memory O(4n). State the invariant (`sm[o]` is always correct for node o itself; only the children may be stale), and that forgetting to push down before recursing, or using `v` instead of `v * length`, are the classic bugs. Segment-tree recursion depth is about log2(n) (under 20 here), far below CPython's default recursion limit of 1000, so recursion is fine. A shorter alternative for sum-only is two Fenwick trees; the difference-array version for offline updates is Part 13 Q4. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]], 'ops': [['sum', [0, 4]], ['add', [1, 3, 10]], ['sum', [0, 4]], ['sum', [2, 2]], ['add', [0, 4, -1]], ['sum', [0, 1]], ['sum', [3, 4]]]}, [15, None, 45, 13, None, 11, 17])],'Segment-tree and Fenwick-tree interview-pattern guides (several techinterview.org posts, a mintlify Fenwick snippet page, cp-algorithms sparse-table page, designgurus range-minimum-query lesson; search snippets only, not opened)'),
(1,'🔴','bit_2d','Two-dimensional Fenwick tree. Implement class `NumMatrix2D(matrix)` (m x n with 1 <= m, n <= 200) with `update(r, c, v)` (set matrix[r][c] = v; returns None) and `sumRegion(r1, c1, r2, c2)` (the sum of the rectangle with corners (r1, c1) and (r2, c2), both included). Keep a 2D tree `t` of size (m + 1) x (n + 1): an update adds the DELTA (new value minus the stored old value) at every (i, j) with the loops `i += i & -i` over rows and `j += j & -j` over columns, a prefix query P(r, c) sums with `i -= i & -i`, `j -= j & -j`, and a region is P(r2+1, c2+1) - P(r1, c2+1) - P(r2+1, c1) + P(r1, c1) (inclusion-exclusion). Both operations O(log m * log n); the build can simply call `update` for every cell (O(m n log m log n)). This is the Fenwick answer promised in Part 13 Q40 (row prefix sums there cost O(m) per query). Say when a segment tree of segment trees or an offline method is preferred (range updates, memory). The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.',
 [({'init': [[[3, 0, 1, 4], [5, 6, 3, 2], [1, 2, 0, 1]]], 'ops': [['sumRegion', [0, 0, 2, 3]], ['sumRegion', [1, 1, 2, 2]], ['update', [1, 2, 10]], ['sumRegion', [1, 1, 2, 2]], ['sumRegion', [1, 2, 1, 2]]]}, [28, 11, None, 18, 10])],'Segment-tree and Fenwick-tree interview-pattern guides (several techinterview.org posts, a mintlify Fenwick snippet page, cp-algorithms sparse-table page, designgurus range-minimum-query lesson; search snippets only, not opened)'),
(1,'🔴','count_smaller','Count of smaller numbers after self. `nums` has 0 <= len <= 10^5 integers (duplicates allowed, |value| <= 10^9). Return a list `res` where res[i] is the number of elements to the RIGHT of position i that are STRICTLY smaller than nums[i]. Compress the values to ranks (Q{Q:coord_compress}), scan from the right, and for each element query the Fenwick prefix count of ranks smaller than its own (rank - 1) before inserting its rank; O(n log n). Explain the scan direction (right to left, so the tree holds exactly the elements to the right) and why the merge-sort alternative has to carry the original indices through the merge (compare Part 13 Q17 and Part 13 Q47 for merge-sort counting). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against an O(n^2) double loop on small inputs.',
 [({'nums': [5, 2, 6, 1]}, [2, 1, 1, 0]), ({'nums': [2, 2, 2]}, [0, 0, 0]), ({'nums': []}, [])],'Segment-tree and Fenwick-tree interview-pattern guides (several techinterview.org posts, a mintlify Fenwick snippet page, cp-algorithms sparse-table page, designgurus range-minimum-query lesson; search snippets only, not opened)'),
(3,'🟡','bit_point_range',"Fenwick tree (BIT): point update and range sum. Implement class `NumArray(nums)` (1 <= len <= 10^5, |value| <= 10^9) with `update(i, val)` (SET nums[i] = val, returns None) and `sumRange(l, r)` (the sum of nums[l..r], both ends included). Keep a Fenwick array `t` of size n + 1 (1-based): `t[i]` stores the sum of the block of length `i & -i` ending at index i. `update` adds the DELTA (new value minus the old value you keep in a separate list) with `while i <= n: t[i] += d; i += i & -i`; a prefix sum uses `while i > 0: s += t[i]; i -= i & -i`; `sumRange(l, r)` = prefix(r + 1) - prefix(l). Both O(log n); the build can be O(n) by pushing each t[i] to its parent i + (i & -i). Explain why the lowest set bit `i & -i` defines the block, why index 0 cannot be used, and why the common bug 'add val instead of val - old' double counts. Compare with the static prefix array of Part 13 Q1 (O(1) query, O(n) update) and with a segment tree (Q{Q:segtree_min}). The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [[1, 3, 5]], 'ops': [['sumRange', [0, 2]], ['update', [1, 2]], ['sumRange', [0, 2]], ['sumRange', [2, 2]]]}, [9, None, 8, 5]), ({'init': [[-4, 7]], 'ops': [['sumRange', [0, 1]], ['update', [0, 10]], ['sumRange', [0, 0]], ['sumRange', [0, 1]]]}, [3, None, 10, 17])],'Segment-tree and Fenwick-tree interview-pattern guides (several techinterview.org posts, a mintlify Fenwick snippet page, cp-algorithms sparse-table page, designgurus range-minimum-query lesson; search snippets only, not opened)'),
(3,'🟢','moving_avg','[Set A, Q1 of 5, 5 min, 10 pts] Moving average from a data stream. Implement class `MovingAverage(size)` (1 <= size <= 1000) with `next(val)` (|val| <= 10^4) that adds `val` to the stream and returns the average (a float, total / count) of the last `size` values, or of all values so far when fewer than `size` have arrived. Keep a deque and a running total (add the new value, subtract the value that falls out), O(1) per call and O(size) memory; say why recomputing sum(last size values) is O(size) per call and why floats are only formed at the end (the integer total is exact). This is a ring-buffer idea (compare the circular queue of Part 4 Q22) and the fixed-window average of Part 2 Q3, but online. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.',
 [({'init': [3], 'ops': [['next', [1]], ['next', [10]], ['next', [3]], ['next', [5]], ['next', [-4]]]}, [1.0, 5.5, 4.666666666666667, 6.0, 1.3333333333333333])],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
(2,'🟡','underground','[Set A, Q2 of 5, 7 min, 15 pts] Underground system (average trip times). Implement class `UndergroundSystem()` with `checkIn(id, station, t)`, `checkOut(id, station, t)` and `getAverageTime(start, end)`. A customer id has at most one open trip at a time; `checkOut` always closes the open trip of that id; the trip duration is t_out - t_in. `getAverageTime` returns the average duration (a float, total / count) over all COMPLETED trips that started at `start` and ended at `end` (direction matters: A to B and B to A are different); it is only called after at least one such trip. Here t is an argument of the call (a trip timestamp), not a live clock. Keep a dict id -> (station, t) for open trips and a dict (start, end) -> [total, count] for finished ones; every operation O(1). Say what you would do when a customer id checks out without having checked in (the checker never does it) and why storing a running total and count beats storing every duration. Hash-map idioms: Part 1 Q39. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.',
 [({'init': [], 'ops': [['checkIn', [45, 'A', 3]], ['checkIn', [32, 'A', 8]], ['checkOut', [45, 'B', 15]], ['checkOut', [32, 'B', 22]], ['getAverageTime', ['A', 'B']], ['checkIn', [10, 'B', 24]], ['checkOut', [10, 'A', 40]], ['getAverageTime', ['B', 'A']], ['checkIn', [45, 'A', 30]], ['checkOut', [45, 'B', 36]], ['getAverageTime', ['A', 'B']]]}, [None, None, None, None, 13.0, None, None, 16.0, None, None, 10.666666666666666])],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
(2,'🟡','leaderboard',"[Set A, Q3 of 5, 9 min, 20 pts] Leaderboard. Implement class `Leaderboard()` with `addScore(player, score)` (add the positive integer `score` to the player's total, creating the player if new; returns None), `top(K)` (the sum of the K highest player totals; when there are fewer than K players, the sum of all of them) and `reset(player)` (remove the player entirely, as if never seen; does nothing for an unknown player; returns None). A player's total can be added to many times. Up to 10^5 operations and 10^4 players. A dict of totals plus `heapq.nlargest(K, totals.values())` gives O(P log K) per `top`; say when that is enough and what you would use if `top` were called far more often than `addScore` (a sorted structure or two heaps with lazy deletion as in Part 8 Q31, or a Fenwick tree over scores). Heap basics are Part 8 Q1 and the kth-largest idea Part 8 Q4. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [], 'ops': [['addScore', [1, 73]], ['addScore', [2, 56]], ['addScore', [3, 39]], ['addScore', [4, 51]], ['addScore', [5, 4]], ['top', [1]], ['reset', [1]], ['reset', [9]], ['addScore', [2, 51]], ['top', [3]], ['top', [10]]]}, [None, None, None, None, None, 73, None, None, None, 197, 201])],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
(3,'🔴','lru_ttl',"[Set A, Q4 of 5, 12 min, 25 pts] LRU cache with expiry (TTL) and an injected clock. Implement class `TTLCache(clock, capacity)` (1 <= capacity <= 1000) with `put(key, value, ttl)` (ttl >= 1 seconds), `get(key)` and `size()`. An entry put at time T with ttl is alive while now < T + ttl and expired from time T + ttl on (exactly at T + ttl it is already gone). `get` returns the value of a live key, or -1 when the key is absent or expired; a successful `get` makes the key the most recently used but does NOT extend its life. `put` first removes every expired entry; then, if the key is already present, replaces its value, ttl and expiry (new life measured from now) and makes it most recently used; otherwise, if the cache already holds `capacity` live keys, evicts the least recently used LIVE key; then inserts. `put` returns None; `size()` returns the number of live entries (after removing expired ones). Combine an OrderedDict (or the list of Q{Q:lru_dll}) for recency with a min-heap of (expiry, key, version) and lazy deletion of stale heap entries (Part 8 Q31): each operation is O(log n) amortised. Say why expired entries must be purged before choosing an LRU victim (an expired entry must never survive while a live one is evicted) and, as a stated concept only, what a lock around the whole method body would protect. The constructor's FIRST argument is `clock`, a zero-argument callable that returns the current time as an integer number of seconds; `init` holds the remaining constructor arguments, and ops are [time, method, [args]] with non-decreasing times: the checker sets the clock to `time` before each call. Read the time only through `clock()`, never from the `time` module. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a naive reference implementation on random operation sequences with an injected clock on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [2], 'ops': [[0, 'put', ['a', 1, 10]], [1, 'put', ['b', 2, 3]], [2, 'get', ['a']], [3, 'put', ['c', 3, 10]], [3, 'get', ['b']], [4, 'size', []], [10, 'get', ['a']], [10, 'size', []], [11, 'put', ['c', 9, 5]], [15, 'get', ['c']], [16, 'get', ['c']]]}, [None, None, 1, None, -1, 2, -1, 1, None, 9, -1])],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
(1,'🔴','scheduler',"[Set A, Q5 of 5, 12 min, 30 pts] Delayed job scheduler with cancellation. Implement class `Scheduler(clock)` with `submit(job_id, delay, priority)` (the job becomes ready at time now + delay, delay >= 0; smaller `priority` numbers are more urgent; a job id is never submitted while a job with the same id is still pending, but an id may be reused after it was polled or cancelled; returns None), `cancel(job_id)` (True when a pending job was removed, else False) and `poll(limit)` which removes and returns, in order, up to `limit` pending jobs that are ready (ready time <= now). Order: smaller ready time first, then smaller priority, then earlier submission. Jobs that are not yet ready are never returned, and a poll with nothing ready returns []. Use a heap of (ready, priority, seq, job_id) and a dict job_id -> seq of live jobs: `cancel` deletes from the dict only (lazy deletion: Part 8 Q31) and `poll` discards heap entries whose seq no longer matches; tie keys follow Part 8 Q29. Submit O(log n), cancel O(1), poll O(k log n) amortised. Explain why the `seq` check (not just the id) is needed when an id is reused after cancel. The constructor's FIRST argument is `clock`, a zero-argument callable that returns the current time as an integer number of seconds; `init` holds the remaining constructor arguments, and ops are [time, method, [args]] with non-decreasing times: the checker sets the clock to `time` before each call. Read the time only through `clock()`, never from the `time` module. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a naive reference implementation on random operation sequences with an injected clock on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [], 'ops': [[0, 'submit', ['a', 5, 2]], [0, 'submit', ['b', 5, 1]], [0, 'submit', ['c', 0, 9]], [0, 'poll', [5]], [4, 'poll', [5]], [5, 'cancel', ['b']], [5, 'poll', [1]], [5, 'submit', ['b', 0, 3]], [5, 'poll', [5]], [6, 'cancel', ['a']], [6, 'poll', [5]]]}, [None, None, None, ['c'], [], True, ['a'], None, ['b'], False, []])],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
(3,'🟢','valid_tree',"[Set B, Q1 of 5, 5 min, 10 pts] Is this graph a tree? `n` nodes 0..n-1 (1 <= n <= 10^5) and `edges`, a list of undirected [u, v] pairs (0 <= len <= 2*10^5; repeats and self loops may occur). Return True exactly when the graph is a tree: connected with no cycle. A parallel edge and a self loop are cycles. Union-find (Part 9 Q13): a tree on n nodes has exactly n - 1 edges, so return False at once for any other count, then union the endpoints and return False when an edge joins two nodes that already share a root. Say why n - 1 edges plus 'no cycle' implies connectedness, and how it differs from Part 9 Q14 (which finds the extra edge in an otherwise tree-like graph). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a BFS connectivity check combined with the edge-count identity on small inputs.",
 [({'n': 5, 'edges': [[0, 1], [0, 2], [0, 3], [1, 4]]}, True), ({'n': 5, 'edges': [[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 3], [1, 3]]}, False), ({'n': 1, 'edges': []}, True), ({'n': 2, 'edges': [[0, 1], [0, 1]]}, False)],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
(2,'🟡','reverse_delete','[Set B, Q2 of 5, 8 min, 15 pts] Components after deleting edges one by one. `n` nodes 0..n-1 (1 <= n <= 10^5), `edges` a list of undirected [u, v] pairs (0 <= len <= 2*10^5) numbered by position, and `removals` a list of DISTINCT edge indices giving the order in which edges are deleted. Return a list of len(removals) + 1 integers: the number of connected components before any deletion and after each deletion in turn. Process OFFLINE in reverse: start with all never-deleted edges in a union-find (Part 9 Q13), record the component count, then add the removed edges back from the last removal to the first, recording the count after each, and reverse the list; deleting is hard for union-find, adding is easy. O((n + m) alpha(n)). Compare with the online union-find of Part 9 Q43 and the plain component count of Part 9 Q3. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against an oracle that recounts the components from scratch after every deletion on small inputs.',
 [({'n': 4, 'edges': [[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 3]], 'removals': [1, 0]}, [1, 2, 3]), ({'n': 3, 'edges': [[0, 1], [0, 1], [1, 2]], 'removals': [0, 1, 2]}, [1, 1, 2, 3])],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
(2,'🟡','connect_cities','[Set B, Q3 of 5, 9 min, 20 pts] Connect all cities, some roads already built. `n` cities 0..n-1 (1 <= n <= 10^4), `built`, a list of [u, v] roads that already exist (cost 0), and `edges`, a list of [u, v, cost] candidate new roads (1 <= cost <= 10^6, up to 5*10^4). Return the minimum total cost of new roads so that every pair of cities is connected, or -1 when impossible even with every candidate. Kruskal (Part 9 Q29): first union the already built roads for free, then take candidate roads in increasing cost order when they join two different components (union-find, Part 9 Q13); stop counting when one component is left. O(E log E). Explain why pre-merging the free roads keeps the greedy proof valid (contracting zero-cost edges) and why Prim would need the same pre-contraction. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against an exhaustive oracle that tries every subset of candidate roads (at most 10 candidates) on small inputs.',
 [({'n': 4, 'built': [[0, 1]], 'edges': [[1, 2, 5], [2, 3, 1], [0, 3, 9], [0, 2, 4]]}, 5), ({'n': 3, 'built': [], 'edges': [[0, 1, 2]]}, -1), ({'n': 1, 'built': [], 'edges': []}, 0)],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
(2,'🔴','redundant_II','[Set B, Q4 of 5, 11 min, 25 pts] Redundant directed connection. `edges` is a list of n directed [parent, child] pairs over nodes 1..n (3 <= n <= 1000, no repeats, no self loops). It was a rooted tree (one root, every other node with exactly one parent, all nodes reachable from the root) plus ONE extra directed edge. Return the edge to remove so that the remaining n - 1 edges form a rooted tree again; if several edges work, return the one that appears LAST in `edges`. Two cases: a node with two parents (take the two candidate edges; try ignoring the later one and look for a cycle with union-find: a cycle means the earlier candidate is the answer, otherwise the later one), and no node with two parents (then there is a cycle and the answer is the last edge that closes it in union-find order, as in the undirected Part 9 Q14). Explain why a node with two parents plus a cycle forces the earlier candidate. O(n alpha(n)). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against an oracle that tries to drop each edge from the last to the first and tests whether the rest is a rooted tree on small inputs.',
 [({'edges': [[1, 2], [1, 3], [2, 3]]}, [2, 3]), ({'edges': [[1, 2], [2, 3], [3, 4], [4, 1], [1, 5]]}, [4, 1]), ({'edges': [[2, 1], [3, 1], [4, 2], [1, 4]]}, [2, 1])],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
(1,'🔴','min_sources','[Set B, Q5 of 5, 12 min, 30 pts] Fewest starting nodes to reach everything. `n` nodes 0..n-1 (1 <= n <= 10^5) and `edges`, a list of directed [u, v] pairs (0 <= len <= 2*10^5; repeats and self loops allowed). Choose the smallest set of start nodes so that every node is reachable from at least one chosen node. Return that smallest set as a sorted list, and when several smallest sets exist return the lexicographically smallest one (compare the sorted lists). Condense the graph with the strongly connected components of Q{Q:scc_kosaraju}: the answer needs exactly one node from every component that has NO incoming edge from another component (a source component), and the smallest node of each source component gives the lexicographically smallest set. Explain why a non-source component is never needed and why one node per source component is enough (Part 9 Q3 counts undirected components; here direction matters). O(n + m) with an iterative DFS. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against an exhaustive oracle that tries all subsets in order of size and lexicographic order (n <= 8) on small inputs.',
 [({'n': 5, 'edges': [[0, 1], [1, 0], [1, 2], [3, 2], [3, 4]]}, [0, 3]), ({'n': 3, 'edges': [[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 0]]}, [0]), ({'n': 3, 'edges': []}, [0, 1, 2])],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
(2,'🟡','range_freq','[Set C, Q1 of 4, 8 min, 15 pts] Range frequency queries. Implement class `RangeFreqQuery(arr)` (1 <= len <= 10^5, 1 <= value <= 10^4) with `query(left, right, value)` returning how many times `value` occurs in arr[left..right] (both ends included, 0 <= left <= right < len). Store, for each value, the sorted list of its positions (Part 1 style dict of lists); a query is two bisect calls: `bisect_right(pos, right) - bisect_left(pos, left)` (bounds semantics: Part 5 Q11, and the first/last position idea of Part 5 Q4), O(log k) per query and O(n) memory; a value that never occurs counts 0. Say why a prefix-count table per value would use O(n * distinct) memory and when it is still the right choice. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.',
 [({'init': [[12, 33, 4, 56, 22, 2, 34, 33, 22, 12, 34, 56]], 'ops': [['query', [1, 2, 4]], ['query', [0, 11, 33]], ['query', [3, 3, 56]], ['query', [0, 11, 99]], ['query', [4, 8, 22]]]}, [1, 2, 1, 0, 2])],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
(1,'🔴','dyn_multiset',"[Set C, Q2 of 4, 11 min, 25 pts] Order statistics on a dynamic multiset with a Fenwick tree. Implement class `OrderStats(max_value)` (1 <= max_value <= 10^6) storing a multiset of integers in [0, max_value], with `insert(x)` (returns None), `remove(x)` (removes ONE copy; returns True when a copy existed, else False), `count_le(x)` (the number of stored elements <= x; 0 for negative x and the full size for x >= max_value) and `kth(k)` (the k-th smallest element counting duplicates, 1-based, or -1 when k < 1 or k > size). Keep a Fenwick tree over the values shifted by +1. `kth` uses binary descent on the tree: start with the largest power of two <= n as `step`, and for each step try `pos + step`; if `t[pos + step] < k`, move there and subtract that count from k; the answer is the final `pos` (the value, because indices are shifted by one). All operations O(log max_value). Explain why the descent beats a binary search over `count_le` (O(log n) instead of O(log^2 n)) and why this is the order-statistics trick behind 'median of a changing multiset' (compare Part 8 Q17). Fenwick basics: Q{Q:bit_point_range}. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [10], 'ops': [['insert', [5]], ['insert', [3]], ['insert', [5]], ['insert', [9]], ['count_le', [5]], ['kth', [1]], ['kth', [3]], ['kth', [4]], ['kth', [5]], ['remove', [5]], ['remove', [7]], ['kth', [3]], ['count_le', [-1]], ['count_le', [10]]]}, [None, None, None, None, 3, 3, 5, 9, -1, True, False, 9, 0, 3])],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
(2,'🔴','reverse_pairs','[Set C, Q3 of 4, 13 min, 30 pts] Reverse pairs. `nums` has 0 <= len <= 5*10^4 integers (|value| <= 2^31, duplicates allowed). Count the pairs i < j with nums[i] > 2 * nums[j] (strict). Python has no overflow, so 2 * nums[j] needs no care here (a 32-bit language would need 64-bit arithmetic: say it). Fenwick solution: coordinate-compress the values (Q{Q:coord_compress}); scan j from left to right; the number of earlier elements greater than 2 * nums[j] is j minus the number of earlier elements <= 2 * nums[j], found with `bisect_right` of 2 * nums[j] in the sorted distinct values and a BIT prefix count; then insert nums[j]. The query key 2 * nums[j] is NOT one of the stored values, which is why you bisect instead of looking up a rank. O(n log n). Compare with the merge-sort counter of Part 13 Q17 and Part 13 Q47 and with Q{Q:inversions_bit}. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against an O(n^2) double loop on small inputs.',
 [({'nums': [1, 3, 2, 3, 1]}, 2), ({'nums': [2, 4, 3, 5, 1]}, 3), ({'nums': [-5, -5]}, 1), ({'nums': []}, 0)],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
(1,'🔴','calendar_k','[Set C, Q4 of 4, 13 min, 30 pts] Maximum overlap after every booking (My Calendar III). `bookings` is a list of up to 400 [start, end) pairs with 0 <= start < end <= 10^9, HALF-OPEN: two bookings that only touch (one ends exactly where the next starts) do not overlap. Return a list with one integer per booking: after adding bookings[0..i], the largest number of bookings that cover a single moment. Offline solution: compress all endpoints (Q{Q:coord_compress}) into elementary cells [xs[k], xs[k+1]), then use a segment tree with lazy range add and global maximum (Q{Q:lazy_range}; the root max is the answer; here `mx[o] = max(children) + lazy[o]` needs no push-down). O(n log n) overall. Compare with the difference-array idea of Part 13 Q4 (which needs small coordinates) and with the sweep of Part 8 Q7 (one answer for the whole set, not after every insert). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against an oracle that recomputes the maximum overlap from scratch after every booking on small inputs.',
 [({'bookings': [[10, 20], [50, 60], [10, 40], [5, 15], [5, 10], [25, 55]]}, [1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3]), ({'bookings': [[0, 5], [5, 10], [0, 10]]}, [1, 1, 2])],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
(3,'🟡','stock_price',"[Set D, Q1 of 4, 8 min, 15 pts] Stock price fluctuation. Implement class `StockPrice()` receiving a stream of (timestamp, price) records that can arrive out of order and can CORRECT an earlier record: `update(timestamp, price)` (returns None) sets the price for that timestamp, replacing any earlier price for the same timestamp. `current()` returns the price at the LATEST timestamp seen so far, `maximum()` and `minimum()` the highest and lowest price over all timestamps using each timestamp's latest price. They are only called after at least one update. Keep a dict timestamp -> price and two heaps of (price, timestamp) (negate for the max); on `maximum` and `minimum` discard heap tops whose price no longer equals the dict value (lazy deletion, Part 8 Q31; the changing-priority idea of Part 8 Q38). Amortised O(log n) per call; say why every pushed entry is discarded at most once. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [], 'ops': [['update', [1, 10]], ['update', [2, 5]], ['current', []], ['maximum', []], ['update', [1, 3]], ['maximum', []], ['update', [4, 2]], ['minimum', []], ['current', []], ['update', [4, 20]], ['maximum', []], ['minimum', []]]}, [None, None, 5, 10, None, 5, None, 2, 2, None, 20, 3])],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
(2,'🟡','top_voted',"[Set D, Q2 of 4, 10 min, 25 pts] Leader at any past time (online election). Implement class `TopVotedCandidate(persons, times)`: vote i was cast for `persons[i]` at time `times[i]` (strictly increasing times, 1 <= len <= 5000, persons are small integers). `q(t)` (with t >= times[0]) returns the person who is leading at time t counting all votes cast at times <= t; if several people tie for the lead, the one whose most recent vote is the latest wins. Precompute in the constructor the leader after each vote (a Counter and the rule `count[p] >= count[leader]` makes the newest voter win ties), then answer `q(t)` with `bisect_right(times, t) - 1` (bounds semantics: Part 5 Q11), O(log n) per query. Say why a tie goes to the person who just voted and what changes if the rule were 'smallest id wins'. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a simple reference implementation (a naive version that rescans plain lists) on random operation sequences on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [[0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1, 0], [0, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30]], 'ops': [['q', [3]], ['q', [12]], ['q', [25]], ['q', [15]], ['q', [24]], ['q', [8]]]}, [0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1])],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
(3,'🔴','freq_stack',"[Set D, Q3 of 4, 13 min, 30 pts] Maximum frequency stack. Implement class `FreqStack()` with `push(x)` (returns None) and `pop()`, which removes and returns the most frequent element currently in the stack; if several elements share the highest frequency, remove the one whose TOP-most occurrence is closest to the top of the stack (the most recently pushed among them). `pop` is never called on an empty stack. O(1) per operation: a dict value -> count, a dict count -> stack of values (the value is pushed on stack f when its count becomes f), and `max_freq`; `pop` takes the top of stack[max_freq], decrements that value's count and lowers `max_freq` when its stack empties. Explain why one value can sit on several frequency stacks at once and why this gives exactly the tie rule. The related designs are the min stack of Part 4 Q2 and the max stack of Part 4 Q38; top-k frequencies are Part 1 Q10. The checker builds the object with `init` (the constructor arguments), then runs `ops`, a list of [method, [args]] calls in order, and collects one return value per call (None for a method that returns nothing). In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against a naive reference that scans the whole stack for the highest frequency and its topmost occurrence on small inputs.",
 [({'init': [], 'ops': [['push', [5]], ['push', [7]], ['push', [5]], ['push', [7]], ['push', [4]], ['push', [5]], ['pop', []], ['pop', []], ['pop', []], ['pop', []]]}, [None, None, None, None, None, None, 5, 7, 5, 4])],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
(2,'🔴','skyline','[Set D, Q4 of 4, 14 min, 30 pts] The skyline problem. `buildings` is a list of 1 to 10^4 triples [left, right, height] with 0 <= left < right <= 2^31 - 1 and 1 <= height <= 2^31 - 1, sorted by `left` (equal lefts in any order). Return the skyline as the list of key points [x, height] from left to right: a key point is the left end of a horizontal segment, i.e. the x where the maximum height of the buildings covering x changes; the last key point has height 0 at the right end of the right-most building; no two consecutive key points may have the same height, and a gap between buildings shows as a drop to 0 and a rise again. Sweep events in sorted order (starts with (x, -height), ends with (x, 0), so starts at one x are handled before ends and taller starts first), keep a max-heap of (-height, right end) and pop the top lazily while its right end is <= x (Part 8 Q31); emit a point whenever the current maximum changes. O(n log n). Say why touching buildings [0, 2, 3] and [2, 4, 3] must not produce a point at x = 2 (equal heights) and relate the event sweep to Part 8 Q7 and Part 8 Q5. In the answer include a dry run on the first example and a seeded random cross-check of your solution against an oracle that evaluates the maximum covering height at every integer x of a small coordinate range and extracts the changes on small inputs.',
 [({'buildings': [[2, 9, 10], [3, 7, 15], [5, 12, 12], [15, 20, 10], [19, 24, 8]]}, [[2, 10], [3, 15], [7, 12], [12, 0], [15, 10], [20, 8], [24, 0]]), ({'buildings': [[0, 2, 3], [2, 4, 3]]}, [[0, 3], [4, 0]]), ({'buildings': [[1, 5, 4], [1, 3, 6], [3, 7, 2]]}, [[1, 6], [3, 4], [5, 2], [7, 0]])],'Mock-set design by the editor in the style of timed online-assessment and onsite rounds at product companies (no source reports these exact sets); the individual problems are standard public-list problems known to the editor, indicative only, no company attribution'),
